12.Use Iris flower dataset and perform following :
1. Create a box plot for each feature in the dataset.
2. Identify and discuss distributions and identify outliers from them.


# program12_iris_boxplot.py
# Iris dataset: boxplots + distribution/outlier discussion

from sklearn.datasets import load_iris
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

iris = load_iris()
data = iris.data
feature_names = iris.feature_names

# 1) Create a box plot for each feature
for i in range(data.shape[1]):
    plt.figure()
    plt.boxplot(data[:, i])
    plt.title(f"Box Plot of {feature_names[i]}")
    plt.ylabel(feature_names[i])
    plt.tight_layout()
    plt.show()

# 2) Printing distribution & outlier analysis (based on visual inspection)
print("---- Distribution & Outlier Notes ----\n")
print("1) Sepal length (cm):")
print("   - Distribution: fairly symmetric.")
print("   - Outliers: boxplot generally shows none or very few.\n")

print("2) Sepal width (cm):")
print("   - Distribution: slightly skewed.")
print("   - Outliers: usually a few visible (high-end or low-end).\n")

print("3) Petal length (cm):")
print("   - Distribution: strongly separated groups (because of 3 species).")
print("   - Outliers: typically none, values form distinct clusters.\n")

print("4) Petal width (cm):")
print("   - Distribution: also shows clear class separation.")
print("   - Outliers: normally none, values lie in three clean bands.\n")
